# Universe content · physics: forces, atoms, planet interiors, atmospheres
# researched: OpenStax College Physics "The Four Basic Forces"; Manchester nuclear physics notes;
#   geodynamo and mantle-convection reviews (arXiv 1304.2437, 1005.3523, 1910.14022);
#   planetary atmosphere study guides (structure, escape, greenhouse); Wikipedia "Atmosphere"
# Spec: docs/CONTENT-FORMAT.md · wiki/systems/r03-engine-core.md · wiki/systems/r06-planet.md

# ── The four forces of nature ──

@force gravity
name: Gravity
real: yes
strength: weakest of the four
range: endless
pulls-or-pushes: only pulls
acts-on: everything with mass or energy
carrier: graviton (hypothetical, never observed)
does: holds planets, stars and galaxies together; makes orbits, tides and black holes; decides whether a world keeps its air

@force electromagnetism
name: Electromagnetism
real: yes
strength: second strongest
range: endless
pulls-or-pushes: both
acts-on: anything with electric charge
carrier: photon
does: light, electricity, magnetism, lightning, and every chemical bond that holds molecules and life together

@force strong-nuclear
name: Strong nuclear force
real: yes
strength: the strongest
range: tiny, inside an atom's nucleus
carrier: gluon
does: glues quarks into protons and neutrons, and holds the nucleus together; powers fusion in stars and fission in reactors and bombs

@force weak-nuclear
name: Weak nuclear force
real: yes
strength: weak, very short-ranged
range: smaller than a proton
carrier: W and Z particles
does: radioactive decay, and turns one kind of particle into another; helps stars burn

# ── The laws the matter is built by (numbers the game uses) ──
# nuclear-force: the strong force between two nucleons (protons and neutrons): a hard core where they push apart, a pull
#   just beyond it, nothing past a few fm (they sit about 1.8 fm apart: nuclear matter's density, 0.16 nucleons per fm3).
# coulomb: the electric push between two protons, k = 1.44 MeV fm (e squared over 4 pi epsilon-0).
# mass-formula: the semi-empirical (Bethe-Weizsacker) binding energy of a nucleus, MeV: volume (the strong force between
#   neighbours), surface (fewer neighbours at the edge), coulomb (the protons' push), asymmetry (the Pauli principle,
#   protons and neutrons in balance), pairing (pairs bind tighter). Textbook values.
# beta-stability: the number of protons a nucleus of A nucleons is most stable with, Z = A / (2 + c A^(2/3)), c = 0.0155;
#   fewer protons than that and a neutron turns into a proton (beta-minus decay, the weak force: an electron and an
#   antineutrino out); more and a proton turns into a neutron (beta-plus or electron capture); past lead (Z above 82)
#   the biggest nuclei throw out alpha particles (two protons, two neutrons) as the electric push outgrows the strong pull.
# gravity: G times one solar mass (the pull of a star of one solar mass, m3/s2, known far better than G itself); orbits follow Kepler's third law
#   from it (a year squared is the orbit in AU cubed over the star's mass in solar masses), a pull falls with the distance squared
#   (Newton), and a black hole's horizon is 2GM/c2 (Schwarzschild: about 2.95 km for each solar mass). earth-g: Earth's pull at
#   its ground, to compare with. light: the speed of light in empty space.
@law gravity
name: Gravity's pull
gm-sol-m3-s2: 1.32712e20
g-m3-kg-s2: 6.674e-11
earth-g-m-s2: 9.81

# tidal-lock: a body orbiting close to another is slowed by its pull until one face always points at it (our Moon at the
#   Earth, most moons at their planets, worlds close to dim stars at their star); its day is then its year. Within about
#   lock-au-sol AU of a star of one solar mass a world is locked over billions of years, the reach growing with the cube root
#   of the star's mass (Gladman et al. 1996, Kasting et al. 1993: red dwarfs' habitable zones lie inside it).
# orbits: no orbit is a perfect circle; each follows from the body's speed where it was placed, as the laws of motion have
#   it: a share f of the circular speed there (1 a circle; a little off an oval; near root 2, escape speed, a long thin loop
#   flown ultra-fast through its closest point and crawled through its farthest). Faster than circular the place is its
#   closest point (a = r / (2 - f^2), e = f^2 - 1), slower its farthest (e = 1 - f^2); its size, period and speed everywhere
#   follow. planet-speed-spread: how far most planets' speeds stray from circular (a spread; the Solar System's ovals of 0.01
#   to 0.21); wild-share and wild-speed: the share flung into long loops and how fast (1.15 to 1.38: ovals of 0.3 to 0.9, as
#   many round other stars); moon-close-speed and moon-far-speed for moons near and far (beyond moon-far-radii of their
#   planet, captured, stretched). Relativity turns each orbit's closest point round the star by 6 pi G M / (c^2 a (1 - e^2))
#   an orbit (Mercury: 43 arcseconds a century; near a black hole whole turns each orbit), and clocks run slower deep in a
#   gravity well by root (1 - 2 G M / (r c^2)).
@law orbits
name: The shapes of orbits, from their speeds
planet-speed-spread: 0.06
wild-share: 0.08
wild-speed: 1.15..1.38
moon-close-speed: 0.02
moon-far-speed: 1.05..1.3
moon-far-radii: 25

@law tidal-lock
name: Tidal locking
lock-au-sol: 0.5

@law light
name: The speed of light
speed-m-s: 2.998e8

# frame-dragging: a spinning mass drags space round with it (Lense-Thirring; measured round Earth by Gravity Probe B).
#   Every black hole spins, born from a spinning star or fed by a spinning disc: its spin a is a share of the most it could
#   have (1), taken from spin-range by its place. Its horizon pulls in to r+ = M (1 + root(1 - a^2)) (M = GM/c^2; a hole at
#   rest has r+ = 2M, the Schwarzschild radius) and its ergosphere reaches out to 2M at the equator, where space itself is
#   swept round faster than light and nothing can stand still; just outside the horizon space turns at a c / (2 r+). The
#   disc's innermost stable orbit comes in with the spin, from 6M at rest to 1.24M near the most (Bardeen, Press and
#   Teukolsky 1972), so a fast spinner's disc reaches closer in and shines hotter.
@law frame-dragging
name: Frame dragging: a spinning mass drags space round with it
real: yes
spin-range: 0.1..0.998
says: a spinning mass drags space round with it; round a spinning black hole nothing can stand still inside its ergosphere, and its disc reaches closer in the faster it spins

# gravity-off: what happens when gravity is switched off (the Time menu): every body carries on in a straight line at the
#   speed it had (its orbit's speed where it was), every star's own pressure, unopposed, blows it apart, growing at about
#   its sound speed: it doubles in size in about its sound-crossing time (a star of one solar mass in about an hour), which is
#   about swell-over-freefall times its free-fall time root(R^3 / GM); a galaxy's stars fly off on their tangents at the
#   speed they circled at (rim-speed-km-s in @law cosmos), those of a round galaxy every way.
# hole-inside: the way in from the event horizon to the singularity, by the hole's mass: inside there is near-infinite space and
#   no space at once, and the way down the funnel is millions of light-years (way-in-ly for a hole of way-in-mass-sol solar
#   masses, scaling with the mass to the power way-in-power: 1 means in proportion), run in forty tenfolds of the fall's own
#   measure. The fall's card counts the way in against it.
@law hole-inside
name: Inside a black hole: the way in
real: hypothetical
way-in-ly: 3000000
way-in-mass-sol: 10
way-in-power: 1
says: from the event horizon to the singularity is millions of light-years for a stellar black hole and more for a heavier one, by its mass; space inside stretches without end and closes to nothing

@law gravity-off
name: Gravity switched off
real: yes
swell-over-freefall: 2
says: without gravity every body goes straight on at its speed, every star is blown apart by its own pressure in about an hour, and a galaxy's stars fly off on their tangents

@law nuclear-force
name: The strong force between nucleons
core-fm: 1.7
spacing-fm: 1.8
range-fm: 2.6

@law coulomb
name: The electric force between two protons
k-mev-fm: 1.44

@law mass-formula
name: Semi-empirical mass formula
volume-mev: 15.75
surface-mev: 17.8
coulomb-mev: 0.711
asymmetry-mev: 23.7
pairing-mev: 11.18

@law beta-stability
name: The valley of beta stability
c: 0.0155
alpha-above-z: 82

# ── Particles and atoms ──

@particle up-quark
name: Up quark
real: yes
says: one of the two quarks that build protons and neutrons

@particle down-quark
name: Down quark
real: yes
says: the other quark in protons and neutrons

@particle proton
name: Proton
real: yes
says: two up quarks and one down; positive charge; the number of protons decides which element an atom is

@particle neutron
name: Neutron
real: yes
says: one up quark and two down; no charge; changes the atom's isotope

@particle electron
name: Electron
real: yes
says: negative charge, circling the nucleus; shared or swapped electrons make chemical bonds

@particle neutrino
name: Neutrino
real: yes
says: a nearly massless particle made by nuclear reactions, passing through almost everything

@particle photon
name: Photon
real: yes
says: a particle of light, the carrier of electromagnetism

@particle gluon
name: Gluon
real: yes
says: the carrier of the strong force

@particle w-z
name: W and Z particles
real: yes
says: the carriers of the weak force

@particle graviton
name: Graviton
real: hypothetical
says: the proposed carrier of gravity, never observed

@atom nucleus
name: Nucleus
real: yes
says: protons and neutrons packed at the atom's center, held by the strong force

@atom electron-shells
name: Electron shells
real: yes
says: the layers electrons sit in; the outer shell decides how an element reacts

@atom isotope
name: Isotope
real: yes
says: the same element with a different number of neutrons

@atom radioactive-decay
name: Radioactive decay
real: yes
says: an unstable nucleus breaks down over time, through the weak force or otherwise, giving off radiation

@atom fusion
name: Fusion
real: yes
says: light nuclei join into heavier ones and release energy; it powers stars

@atom fission
name: Fission
real: yes
says: heavy nuclei split and release energy; it powers reactors and atomic bombs

@atom chemical-bond
name: Chemical bond
real: yes
says: atoms joined by sharing or swapping electrons; the root of all chemistry and life

# ── Inside a planet ──

@interior inner-core
name: Inner core
real: yes
says: solid metal at the very center (on Earth, iron and nickel)

@interior outer-core
name: Outer core
real: yes
says: liquid metal around the inner core; when it churns and the planet spins, it makes the magnetic field

@interior mantle
name: Mantle
real: yes
says: hot rock that flows very slowly, carrying heat up from the core

@interior crust
name: Crust
real: yes
says: the thin rocky skin we live on

@interior lithosphere
name: Lithosphere
real: yes
says: the stiff outer shell of crust and upper mantle, broken into plates on Earth

@interior magma
name: Magma
real: yes
says: melted rock under the surface; it becomes lava when it erupts

@interior magma-ocean
name: Magma ocean
real: yes
says: a whole planet surface of molten rock, common in young and very hot worlds

# ── What moves inside a planet ──

@process mantle-convection
name: Mantle convection
real: yes
says: hot rock rises and cool rock sinks in slow loops, moving heat out of the planet

@process plate-tectonics
name: Plate tectonics
real: yes
says: the crust broken into moving plates that collide, split and slide, building mountains, trenches, volcanoes and quakes; Earth is the only known planet with it
does: recycles the crust and helps keep the climate and the magnetic field going

@process stagnant-lid
name: Stagnant lid
real: yes
says: one unbroken rigid shell with no moving plates; heat escapes slowly through it (Mars, Venus)

@process volcanism
name: Volcanism
real: yes
says: magma reaching the surface through volcanoes, rifts and hot spots, releasing gases into the air

@process geodynamo
name: Geodynamo
real: yes
says: a churning liquid-metal core plus a spinning planet make a magnetic field; it can stop and restart over billions of years
needs: an electrically conductive liquid core, and rotation

@process magnetic-field
name: Magnetic field
real: yes
says: the planet's field, from its dynamo; it can flip north and south over time
has-one: Earth, Mercury, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune
has-none: Venus, Mars

@process magnetosphere
name: Magnetosphere
real: yes
says: the bubble a magnetic field makes around a planet, turning away fast particles that are harmful to life

@process aurora
name: Aurora
real: yes
says: glowing lights near the poles where particles from the star hit the air

# ── Atmospheres ──

@atmosphere troposphere
name: Troposphere
real: yes
says: the lowest layer, holding most of the air and all the weather; it gets colder as you go up

@atmosphere stratosphere
name: Stratosphere
real: yes
says: above the weather; on Earth it holds the ozone layer

@atmosphere mesosphere
name: Mesosphere
real: yes
says: the cold layer where meteors burn up

@atmosphere thermosphere
name: Thermosphere
real: yes
says: thin and very hot; auroras glow here

@atmosphere exosphere
name: Exosphere
real: yes
says: the outer edge, where gas thins out into space

@atmosphere pressure
name: Air pressure
real: yes
says: the weight of the air above; it falls off fast with height

@atmosphere greenhouse
name: Greenhouse effect
real: yes
says: gases like carbon dioxide and water vapor trap heat; Earth averages about 15 °C instead of about −18 °C

@atmosphere runaway-greenhouse
name: Runaway greenhouse
real: yes
says: heat traps more heat until the planet bakes; Venus, about 96% carbon dioxide, is around 460 °C at the surface

@atmosphere thermal-escape
name: Thermal escape
real: yes
says: hot, light gas moves fast enough to leave the planet for good

@atmosphere hydrodynamic-escape
name: Hydrodynamic escape
real: yes
says: strong starlight heats the upper air so much that whole streams of gas blow away, mostly hydrogen

@atmosphere non-thermal-escape
name: Non-thermal escape
real: yes
says: air lost through chemistry, particle hits and charge swaps instead of heat

@atmosphere example-mars
name: The air of Mars
real: yes
makeup: about 95% carbon dioxide, 2.7% nitrogen, 1.6% argon, 0.13% oxygen
says: very thin

@atmosphere example-giants
name: The air of gas giants
real: yes
makeup: mostly hydrogen and helium
says: deep, with no solid ground below

# ── Simulation rules ──

@rule physics-forces
real: rule
says: all four forces run the simulation: gravity for orbits, tides, air and collapse; electromagnetism for light, chemistry and magnetism; the strong force for fusion and fission; the weak force for radioactive decay

@rule physics-elements
real: rule
says: an atom's protons decide its element (all 118); neutrons decide its isotope; unstable isotopes decay over time

@rule physics-gravity-air
real: rule
says: a planet keeps its air if its gravity is strong and its air is cold; small, hot worlds lose it

@rule physics-interior
real: rule
says: every rocky planet gets layers (core, mantle, crust); heat from inside drives convection, then plate tectonics or a stagnant lid, then volcanoes

@rule physics-dynamo
real: rule
says: a liquid metal core plus spin makes a magnetic field; it can switch off and back on; the field shapes auroras and radiation at the surface

@rule physics-tectonics
real: rule
says: moving plates build mountains, trenches, volcano chains and quakes, recycle the crust, and help keep the climate steady

@rule physics-greenhouse
real: rule
says: greenhouse gases warm a planet; too much can run away into a Venus-like world

@rule physics-tech
real: rule
says: civilizations climb the forces in order: fire and chemistry (electromagnetism), electricity and magnetism, then fission and fusion (the strong force), then mastery of gravity for space travel

# ── the gases of airs, each molecule's atoms (so an air's make-up gives the atoms you meet going in) ──
# number-density: molecules per cubic centimetre at the ground or cloud tops (Earth's air at sea level about 2.5e19, by
# the ideal gas law at 1 bar and 15 C); used for every air at about one bar.
@law star-plasma
name: A star's surface (its photosphere): plasma
makeup: hydrogen 91.2%, helium 8.7%, oxygen 0.05%, carbon 0.03%, neon 0.01%, nitrogen 0.007%, iron 0.003%
per-cc: 1e17
says: so hot (thousands of degrees) that the atoms have lost their electrons: bare nuclei and free electrons, about 0.2 micrometres apart (a star's photosphere: about 2e-7 grams a cubic centimetre)

@law air-density
name: Molecules in a cubic centimetre of air at about one bar
number-density: 2.5e19

@gas hydrogen
atoms: H H
@gas helium
atoms: He
@gas methane
atoms: C H H H H
@gas ammonia
atoms: N H H H
@gas nitrogen
atoms: N N
@gas oxygen
atoms: O O
@gas water
atoms: H H O
@gas argon
atoms: Ar
@gas neon
atoms: Ne
@gas krypton
atoms: Kr
@gas xenon
atoms: Xe
@gas radon
atoms: Rn
@gas carbon dioxide
atoms: C O O
@gas carbon monoxide
atoms: C O
@gas sodium
atoms: Na
@gas potassium
atoms: K
@gas silicon monoxide
atoms: Si O

# ── The dark universe: what the galaxies and the horizon show of it ──
# dark-matter-share: how much of a galaxy's mass is dark matter (about 85%: its stars orbit at a flat speed out to the rim,
#   held by the dark halo; without it they would slow outward like planets); rim-speed-km-s: that flat speed for a galaxy like
#   ours; dark-energy-share: how much of the universe's energy is dark energy (about 68%), which speeds the expansion and
#   sets the horizon beyond which light will never arrive; hubble-km-s-mpc: how fast space stretches, per megaparsec.
@law cosmos
name: Dark matter and dark energy
dark-matter-share: 0.85
rim-speed-km-s: 220
dark-energy-share: 0.68
hubble-km-s-mpc: 70

@law supernova
name: Supernovae: a star's death, and what it leaves
real: yes
rate-per-century: 3
core-collapse-share: 0.7
cc-peak-sol: 5e8
ia-peak-sol: 4e9
cc-rise-days: 10
ia-rise-days: 18
plateau-days: 100
cc-ejecta-km-s: 5000
ia-ejecta-km-s: 10000
free-years: 300
remnant-years: 100000
neutron-star-max-sol: 20
kinds: ii-p, ii-l, iin, ib-c, ia, superluminous
says: a galaxy of a hundred billion stars has about three supernovae a century (two to five: the core collapse of a star of eight solar masses or more about two of them, a white dwarf pushed past its limit by a companion about one), so across the observable universe several go off every second; a core-collapse supernova shines at about five hundred million solar luminosities for its hundred-day plateau (absolute magnitude about -17), a white-dwarf one at about four billion (absolute magnitude -19.3, up in eighteen days, halving every twelve after), brighter than most galaxies; the star's matter is flung out at five to ten thousand km/s (a few hundredths of light's speed, the outermost layers up to a tenth of it), free for a few hundred years, then slowed as it sweeps up the gas between the stars (the radius growing as the two-fifths power of the age), seen as a shell for about a hundred thousand years until it merges with that gas; the core of a star of eight to about twenty solar masses becomes a neutron star (the youngest spin fastest, tens of times a second), heavier than that a black hole; a white dwarf's blast leaves nothing but the shell

# ── The kinds of supernova (their shares of all, their light curves, what they leave) ──
# share: of all supernovae; peak-sol: its peak light in solar luminosities; rise-days: to the peak; plateau-days: held near the peak (0 for
# none); halving-days: how fast it fades after; tail-share: the share of the peak the slow radioactive tail (cobalt-56, halving
# every 77 days) starts from; peak-k: the heat of its glowing face at the peak (its colour: the fireball is a hundred thousand
# degrees at the shock's breakout, cooling to this by the peak, to about 5,500 on a plateau and 4,500 after); ejecta-km-s: how
# fast its matter flies out (its glowing face, the photosphere, lags at about six tenths of that, out to about ten billion km,
# then draws back in as the fireball thins to a cloud); colour: its light from afar; leaves: core (a neutron star under about
# twenty solar masses, else a black hole), nothing, or magnetar.

@supernova ii-p
name: Type II-P supernova (plateau)
real: yes
share: 0.33
peak-sol: 5e8
rise-days: 10
plateau-days: 100
halving-days: 8
tail-share: 0.05
peak-k: 6000
ejecta-km-s: 5000
colour: 1 0.85 0.6
leaves: core
says: the commonest: a red supergiant's core collapses, and its thick hydrogen envelope keeps it shining on a plateau for about a hundred days (absolute magnitude about -17)

@supernova ii-l
name: Type II-L supernova (linear)
real: yes
share: 0.07
peak-sol: 8e8
rise-days: 10
plateau-days: 0
halving-days: 20
tail-share: 0.05
peak-k: 7000
ejecta-km-s: 6000
colour: 1 0.9 0.7
leaves: core
says: a core collapse in a star with a thinner hydrogen envelope: no plateau, a steady fade from a brighter peak

@supernova iin
name: Type IIn supernova (narrow lines)
real: yes
share: 0.06
peak-sol: 2e9
rise-days: 20
plateau-days: 60
halving-days: 25
tail-share: 0.1
peak-k: 10000
ejecta-km-s: 4000
colour: 0.8 0.9 1
leaves: core
says: the blast ploughs into gas the star shed before it died: bright for months, blue, much of its light from that crash

@supernova ib-c
name: Type Ib/Ic supernova (stripped)
real: yes
share: 0.24
peak-sol: 4e8
rise-days: 14
plateau-days: 0
halving-days: 15
tail-share: 0.03
peak-k: 8000
ejecta-km-s: 10000
colour: 0.95 0.95 1
leaves: core
says: a star that lost its hydrogen (for Ic its helium too) in winds or to a companion before its core collapsed: fast and fainter, its matter flung out faster

@supernova ia
name: Type Ia supernova (white dwarf)
real: yes
share: 0.29
peak-sol: 4e9
rise-days: 18
plateau-days: 0
halving-days: 12
tail-share: 0.01
peak-k: 10000
ejecta-km-s: 10000
colour: 0.85 0.9 1
leaves: nothing
says: a white dwarf pushed past 1.4 solar masses by a companion and blown apart entirely: the brightest of the common kinds (absolute magnitude -19.3), up in eighteen days, halving every twelve after, alike enough to measure the universe by

@supernova superluminous
name: Superluminous supernova
real: yes
share: 0.002
peak-sol: 2e10
rise-days: 40
plateau-days: 0
halving-days: 50
tail-share: 0.1
peak-k: 12000
ejecta-km-s: 10000
colour: 0.75 0.85 1
leaves: magnetar
says: ten to a hundred times a common supernova's light (absolute magnitude about -21), slow to rise and slow to fade: a very heavy star, its blast lit from inside by the newborn magnetar spinning down; about one in five hundred

